First authors highly thank the reviewer for their valuable comments.We will address the reviewers concerns in below.
>On the other hand the paper cannot be read and understood standing alone as a self-contained piece of work. The entire discussion is heavily dependent on assumed knowledge of the topic, the broader area, and the usual practices of such simulation experiments. It does not try to explain what is the purpose of training, what is supposed to happen in testing, and what composition and generalization mean in this context. Therefore the results, even if it is strong enough to be useful, appeal only to those closely following the topic.

We have included broader explanations to the current draft including intuitive explanations. Below we will further explain the concepts of compositionality and generalization. 

1. Compositionality :- Agents map environmental entities or concepts with symbols while reaching a consensus for the mapping. If agents follow compositionality, agents would connect individual symbols to atomic concepts in the environment, and combine multiple symbols to form composite messages for expressing complex novel concepts, facilitating generalization. For example, \emph{Red Square} is a compositional expression, containing attributes \emph{Color} and \emph{Shape}, constructed by using atomic concepts \emph{Red} and \emph{Square}, which corresponds to the values taken by the two attributes. Hence, whenever an agent observes a novel input, it can break down the input into a set of attributes and corresponding values. An expression describing the input can be constructed by suitably concatenating the symbols representing each value of the attributes.
2. Generalization :- In this context, generalization means how agents could communicate about unseen combinations of attributes and values. It can be measured by test accuracy similar to other cases in machine learning.

The main purpose of training is to steer agents to make their communications compositional. We do this by incorporating a loss term into the original reconstruction loss. Loss is based on the topological similarity, which is a well studied metric of compositionality,  between input space and message space. Our loss term does not require any additional stages to induce compositionality in communications as other methods, which are based on evolutionary linguistics. 

In testing we expect, and in experiments we show, that when agents trained with non-zero external compositional loss, they can converge to higher test accuracies. Moreover, if agents are trained with large compositional losses they emerge languages that are significantly easier to acquire by new learners. 

>The pairing between the object space and the message space is said to be done with some matrices but it is not clear how such matrices are constructed..

We have added additional explanations on the matrices into the current draft. Let's assume three inputs $x1, x2, x3 $ and corresponding messages $m1, m2, m3$.  First, all pairs of inputs and messages are obtained:- $[(x1,x2), (m1,m2)], [(x1,x3), (m1,m3)], [(x2,x3), (m2,m3)]$. Next, cosine distance between each pair can be calculated as: $[1-cos(x1,x2), 1-cos(m1,m2)], [1-cos(x1,x3), 1-cos(m1,m3)], [1-cos(x2,x3), 1-cos(m2,m3)].$ Then the distances of each pair is as follows, $[d(x1,x2), d(m1,m2)], [d(x1,x3), d(m1,m3)], [d(x2,x3), d(m2,m3)]$. The topological similarity is defined as the pearson correlation between the two distance vectors : $<d(x1,x2),d(x1,x3),d(x2,x3)> $ and $<d(m1,m2),d(m1,m3),d(m2,m3)>$ . The expression $[1-cos(Ah0, Bh0)]$ and [$1-cos(Am, Bm)] $ directly provide the above two vectors.  

The matrices A and B in the paper permutate the inputs and their messages to construct every possible pairwise formation which is later converted to cosine distances. Topological similarity measures how the distances between points in the input space correlate with the distances of the corresponding points in the message space. 

Lets assume three inputs $X = <x_1, x_2, x_3>^T$, and their corresponding messages $M = <m_1, m_2, m_3>^T$, in a situation where the batch size is equal to $3$. Then we define the matrices $\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{B}$ as follows.

\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{A} = 
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 \\\\
1 & 0 & 0 \\\\
0 & 1 & 0
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{B} = 
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 1 & 0 \\\\
0 & 0 & 1 \\\\
0 & 0 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

Which yields pairing vectors as follows,

\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{A}X = X_A = <x_1, x_1, x_2>^T 
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{B}X = X_B = <x_2, x_3, x_3>^T 
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{A}M = M_A = <m_1, m_1, m_2>^T 
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{B} = M_B = <m_2, m_3, m_3>^T
\end{equation*}

Using vectors $X_A, X_B$, cosine distance between each input pair,

For inputs,
\begin{equation*}
d_x = 1 - cos(X_A - X_B)
\end{equation*}

